"executive_summary": "This study investigates highly concentrated aqueous binary electrolytes composed of 20 mol kg⁻¹ potassium acetate (CH₃COOK) and varying concentrations of sodium acetate (CH₃COONa). The research addresses the limitations of conventional organic electrolytes and standard aqueous systems by exploring the 'water-in-salt' regime to widen the electrochemical stability window (ESW). Four specific compositions were synthesized and characterized using differential scanning calorimetry (DSC), rheology, conductivity measurements, and electrochemical testing. The most promising formulation, containing 7 mol kg⁻¹ sodium acetate (7N20K), exhibited a conductivity of 21.2 mS cm⁻¹ at 25°C and an ESW extending up to 3 V under ideal conditions. This electrolyte was successfully paired with a carbon-coated LiTi₂(PO₄)₃ (CLTP) NASICON electrode to demonstrate its viability for sodium-ion batteries. The cell achieved a first-cycle anodic capacity of 85 mAh g⁻¹, an average charge efficiency of 96% at low currents, and retained 90% of its capacity after 60 cycles. Kinetic analysis revealed a low activation energy for charge transfer (0.12 eV), confirming favorable interface properties. The work provides a fundamental physicochemical understanding of these binary acetate systems, highlighting their potential as safe, low-cost alternatives to fluorinated salts in next-generation batteries.",
